# Untitled

Nanostructure@metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for catalytic carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\right)\) conversion in photocatalysis, electrocatalysis, and thermal catalysis
Haiqing Wang \(\left(\boxtimes\right)\)
Institute for Advanced Interdisciplinary Research (IAIR), University of Jinan, Jinan 250022, China
\(\circledcirc\) Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2021 Received: 23 September 2021 / Revised: 6 November 2021 / Accepted: 7 November 2021
## ABSTRACT
The catalytic conversion of carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\right)\) into high value-added chemicals is of great significance to address the pressing carbon cycle issues. Reticular chemistry of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-based materials exhibits great potential and effectiveness to face \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) challenge from capture to conversion. To date, the integrated nanocomposites of nanostructure and MOF have emerged as a powerful heterogeneous catalysts featured with multifold advantages including synergistic effects between the two interfaces, confinement effect of meso- and micropores, tandem reaction triggered by multiple active sites, high stability and dispersion, and so on. Given burgeoning carbon cycle and nanostructure@MOFs, this review highlights some of important advancements to provide a full understanding on the synthesis and design of nanostructure@MOFs composites to facilitate carbon cycle through \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photocatalytic, electrocatalytic, and thermal conversion. Afterward, the catalytic applications of some representative nanostructure@MOFs composites are categorized, in which the origin of activity or structure-activity relationship is summarized. Finally, the opportunities and challenges are proposed for inspiring the future development of nanostructure@MOFs composites for carbon cycle.
## KEYWORDS
nanostructure@MOFs, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photocatalysis, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) electrocatalysis, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) thermal catalysis
## 1 Introduction
Excessive emission of carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)\) has resulted in increasingly serious global climatic problem. Extensive endeavor has been devoted in different each stages in the carbon cycle from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture, storage and conversion. Thereinto, direct catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion into high value-added chemicals through photocatalysis, electrocatalysis, thermal catalysis and a direct combination of the three catalysis has exhibited great promise to mitigate the pressing carbon cycle problem [1, 2]. Moreover, various \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion products from \(\mathrm{C}_{1},\mathrm{C}_{2},\mathrm{C}_{3}\) to \(\mathrm{C}_{5}\) molecules can be tuned through the transfer of multiple protons and electrons to meet the feedstock requirements of different application scenarios [3–8]. However, the intractable challenge in catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion is the high bond enthalpy \(+805\;\mathrm{kJ}\cdot\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules, signifying that a high energy input is required to break the highly stable \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) bond [9–13].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been extensively studied as a powerful porous materials to address the global energy and environmental issues due to the reticular chemistry induced the significant structural designability and functionality in MOFs [1, 2, 14]. Taking advantage of the free, abundant and renewable energy, the photocatalytic and electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) have been continuously appealing. The exploitation of efficient MOFs photocatalysts to achieve high solar light utilization, the separation and transfer of photogenerated charge carriers, and the adsorption and activation \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules for initiating the
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been extensively studied as a powerful porous materials to address the global energy and environmental issues due to the reticular chemistry induced the significant structural designability and functionality in MOFs [1, 2, 14]. Taking advantage of the free, abundant and renewable energy, the photocatalytic and electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) have been continuously appealing. The exploitation of efficient MOFs photocatalysts to achieve high solar light utilization, the separation and transfer of photogenerated charge carriers, and the adsorption and activation \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules for initiating the reaction is highly attractive [9, 10, 15, 16]. Despite the developments in this field, the efficiency of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction in pure MOF systems is still not satisfactory. Moreover, manipulating the photocatalytic selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to high value-added chemicals is still highly challenging. As for electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reaction \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\right)\), metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been extensively studied as brilliant electrocatalysts. Their highly tunable structure/active site/prop size and remarkable specific surface area are beneficial to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecule enrichment for promoting \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) activity [17–20]. And the well-defined catalytic environments in MOFs were also considered to be a suitable system for catalytic mechanism explanation. The highly tunable secondary building units of metal clusters and organic linkers enable MOFs to be a flexible platform for the design of complex catalyst. However, considering the multi-electron and/or proton involved \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) process, the poor electrical conductivity and the weak electron-donating capability extremely constrain the accomplishment of MOFs being as efficient \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) electrocatalysts. Integrating MOFs with electron-rich and high-conductivity nanostructure with definite active site to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) product will probably develop into an effective strategy for enhancing the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) selectivity and efficiency [10, 17, 18, 21–24]. As for the thermal catalysis of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion, the pure MOFs have been rarely reported due to the lack of active site and the structure stability problem [25, 26]. But the great porosity and structural adjustability of MOFs can not only trigger strong metal-
support interactions (SMSIs) between the active nanostructures and the orthogonal metal-coordinating sites from the ligands and metal-oxo cluster secondary building units (SBUs) of MOFs, but also emerge the confinement effect by the nanochannels of MOFs [10, 23, 27, 28]. Although several reviews about MOF and MOF-based materials have been published previously, an updated review that focused on nanostructures@MOF materials for \(C O_{2}\) conversion is highly necessary considering the rapid development of nanostructure@MOFs materials.
In this review, we systematically summarize the recent advances of nanostructure@MOFs materials at the atomic and molecular level for heterogeneous \(C O_{2}\) catalytic conversion (Fig. 1). First, the synthesis philosophy of nanostructure@MOFs is presented. Second, the interfacial interaction paradigms of nanostructure@ MOFs are described. Third, the applications of nanostructure@MOFs for catalytic \(C O_{2}\) conversion are fully discussed including photocatalysis (Semiconductor/Metal/Photosensitizer/ Quantum dots/Graphene/POMs/MOF@MOFs), electrocatalysis (Metal/ Metal oxide/ POM/ Nanocarbon/ Metallocene@MOFs), photoelectrocatalysis, and thermal catalysis (Metal/Metal oxid/Cluster/Molecular Complex@MOFs). Fourth, elucidation of structure-activity relationship in nanostructure@MOFs for catalytic \(C O_{2}\) conversion is demonstrated. Challenges and outlooks are discussed at the end of the article aiming at inspiring the rational design of more creative nanostructure@MOFs catalyst for catalytic \(C O_{2}\) conversion.
## 2 Synthesis philosophy of nanostructure@MOF
Although nanostructure@MOF multifunctional materials have recently aroused great interests, the structural dimension between nanoparticles and MOF determines the great challenge in the nanoparticle or MOF overgrowth. Currently, the synthesis of nanostructure@MOF composites can be summarized in four ways [29]. i) Introducing the precursors of nanoparticles into the prepared MOF crystals through vapor-phase deposition or liquid-phase impregnation methods. The nanoparticle crystals within MOFs will be formed after the utilization of further heat/radiation/reducing inducements (Fig. 2(a)). In 2005, the researchers reported the loading of highly porous coordination polymers host lattices though organic-metal chemical vapor deposition to achieve the synthesis of metal@MOF [30]. In 2008, the researchers prepared Cu and ZnO nanostructure@MOF-5 nanocatalyst through gas-phase infiltration and by hydrogenolysis or photoassisted thermolysis with the volatile [CpCuL] \(\left(\mathrm{L}=\mathrm{PMe}_{3},\right.\) CNtBu) and \(Z\mathrm{nEt}_{2}\) as precursors. The catalytic activity of fresh
catalyst can reach about \(60\%\) of the industrial reference at the same reaction conditions (1 atm and \(220\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). Unfortunately, the stability of the catalyst in methanol synthesis is not yet satisfactory, which suffered from structural collapsing and failed performance under several hours' operation [31]. The confinement growths of two-dimensional materials clusters or quantum dots with well-defined atomic structure were also developed. The nickel(II) chloride, nickel(II)/cobalt(II)/iron(II) chloride sheets were successfully immobilized in MOF through coordinating with the well-designed six chelating bipyridine linkers, strongly signifying that the size, structure and spatial dispersion of inorganic low-dimension materials can be well modulated through manipulating the local pore environment of MOF [32]. ii) The most widely applied synthetic methods for nanostructure@MOF composites are based on a 'one-pot' approach in which the MOF encapsulation can be derived in the presence of the functional nanoparticles (Fig. 2(b)). As for the hydrothermal synthesis of nanostructure@MOF materials, the amount of PVP, the ratio of ethanol/DMF, and the crystallization time have been well elucidated in the synthesis of core-shell Pd@IRMOF-3 through a series of control experiments. Normally, PVP is well known as a stabilizer to disperse Pd nanoparticles. In this experiment, the coordination and hydrophobic interactions between PVP and IRMOF-3 precursors can provide suitable affinity to grow MOF shell on the surface of Pd nanoparticles. The ratio of mixed solvent can regulate the nucleation and the subsequent growth of IRMOF-3 shells, thus determining the final core-shell Pd@IRMOF-3 nanostructures. The crystallization time was found to be a very effective method to modulate the thickness of the IRMOF-3 shell [33]. A typical Au@MOF-5 \(C O_{2}\) sensor with controlled size of single Au nanoparticle core and thickness of the MOF-5 shell was prepared by a facile one-pot method, and the \(C O_{2}\) molecules can be selectively detected in the gas mixtures of \(\mathrm{N}_{2},\mathrm{CO},\) and \(C O_{2}\) [34]. iii) The emerging pseudomorphic replication is conceptually different with the above two approaches. A core-shell inorganic particle needs to be prepared in advance in which the core should be the nanoparticle part while the shell materials can play the role of inorganic node feedstock for MOF growth. The MOF encapsulation will be formed by well controlling the reaction between the inorganic node and the organic precursor ligands (Fig. 2(c)). In order to obtain the porous coordination polymer (PCP) crystals with higher-order structure, the shaped nanoparticle cores should play two roles, i.e., metal source and architecture-directing agent for the subsequent spatial organization or morphological replacement of PCP architecture with nanoparticle encapsulation and hierarchical porosity [35]. Moreover, a well-defined nanoparticles MOF interface was prepared by replacing the dynamic dissociated and weakly adsorbed capping agents such as cetyltrimethylammonium surfactants. The MOF shell can grow in situ on the surface of nanoparticles without the trapped capping agent. The resultant nanostructure@ZIF-8 and UiO-66-type MOFs samples show uniform core-shell structure featuring with specific facet alignment. The defined interface of one nanostructure@one MOF nanocrystal provides a high selectivity to unsaturated alcohols in aldehyde catalytic hydrogenation reaction, compared with the conventional preparation method. The strategy is universal to construct aligned and well-defined interfaces between materials with diverse structural dimensions [36]. iv) The MOFs and nanoparticles can be individually prepared, and the nanostructure@MOFs can be achieved by subsequent mixing of the two components (Fig. 2(d)) [29].
The key challenges for the construction of nanostructure@MOFs composites are summarized below [1, 2, 35]. Firstly, the complete encapsulation of nanoparticles within the
MOF framework. Secondly, the approaches that can fully control the size, morphology, interface and composition of nanostructure@MOFs composites are more appealing. Thirdly, the resultant composites have great ability to overcome the nanoparticle agglomeration as well as the structural stability problem. Fourthly, the effective manipulation of different kinds of nanoparticles in the MOF skeleton space will be of greater significance for the future multifunctionality of nanostructure@MOFs.
## 3 Interfacial interaction paradigms of nanostructure@MOF
The regioselectivity in nanostructure@MOF is suggested to be from steric confinement imposed by the porosity of MOF. Density functional theory (DFT) models implied that the diffusion, configuration and interaction types of reaction molecules will be largely changed at the interface between nanoparticle and MOF. The resultant modulation in free energy barrier will lead to the controlled reaction activity and selectivity. Based on the previous reports, the difference of the interaction way (intermediate conformation on a pure metal surface/direct nanoparticle-MOF interface/ill-defined nanoparticle-MOF interface) between the intermediate and the interface in nanostructure@MOF is summarized in Fig. 3. The conformation-selectivity relationship was also well verified through theoretical calculation and experimental tests [37, 38]. The configuration of \(\alpha,\beta\)-unsaturated aldehyde on the pure metal surface shows a flat conformation by \(\pi\)-bond interaction, which results in a low selectivity to unsaturated alcohol (Fig. 3(a)). By contrast, a tilt conformation at the well-defined nanostructure@MOF interface through a di-\(\alpha\) bond of \(\mathrm{CHO}\) moiety produced high selectivity to unsaturated alcohol (Fig. 3(b)). The ill-defined interface usually featured with a gap or capping agents trapped at the nanostructure@MOF interface leading to a weak control over the intermediate conformation, thereby implying an unpredictable catalytic activity and selectivity (Fig. 3(c)) [37]. The different interaction modes usually correspond to the differences in construction methods of nanostructure@MOF interface. As shown in Fig. 3(d), impregnation method is the traditional strategy but can produce a direct nanostructure and MOF interface. As for the coating method, the capping agent stabilized nanostructures were presynthesized, and the presence of the capping agent was also helpful for MOFs anchoring. Unfortunately, the interaction between nanostructure and MOF is indirect owing to the trapped capping agent at the interfaces (Fig. 3(e)). Currently, the one-pot
method exhibited great potential to generate direct nanostructure and MOF interface because of no introduction of capping agent (Fig. 3(f)). However, the preparation condition in one-pot method needs to be well tuned. Although some progress has been made, the research on the interactions among the reactant/intermediate/production, the direct/indirect nanostructure and MOF interface, and the catalytic activity/selectivity is still exciting.
## 4 Catalytic \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) conversion
## 4.1 Photocatalysis
Powered by the free and abundant solar energy, photocatalytic reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into high value-added chemicals is extremely appealing, in which four principal aspects, i.e., light-harvesting, separation and transfer of photo-generated charge carriers, and adsorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules, and proper energy band structure for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) activation. Benefiting from the intrinsic high surface areas and porosity as well as tunable structures, MOFs have exhibited some unique abilities for the light harvesting over broad range, the promoted separation of photo-generated electron-hole pairs, the high accessibility of active sites, the proper platform of identifying the structure-activity relationship, and so on. In addition, the strong \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture capability can endow the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction active sites with highly concentrated/enriched \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules to promote the reaction balance for producing high-value chemicals. The photocatalytic products, such as \(\mathrm{CO}\) and organic chemicals (HCOOH, \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\)), require different redox potentials \(-3.50~\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{CO}\), \(-3.42~\mathrm{eV}\) for HCOOH, \(-3.65~\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\) and \(-3.79~\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) to initiate the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction (Figs. 4(a) and 4(b)) [15, 39]. As for \(\mathrm{CO}\) production, the two-electron photoreduction process implies a relatively low kinetic barrier. The productions of HCOOH, \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) need the transfer of different proton number and 2/6/8 electrons, respectively, which has to overcome higher kinetic barrier. Normally, the highest occupied molecular orbitals (HOMO) and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals (LUMO) of MOF photocatalysts determined the production selectivity, which are mainly manipulated by modulating the organic linker and/or the metal cluster [40]. Unfortunately, the efficiency and the selectivity of pure MOF materials are still very limited. Interestingly, some recent publications have shown that integrating MOF with semiconductors, photosensitizers or metal nanoparticles to construct nanostructure@MOF composites is responsible for the promoted photocatalysis efficiency and the high production
selectivity [17, 41–44]. As shown in Fig. 4(c), the roles of MOF in nanostructure@MOF composites can be classified into photocatalysts, cocatalysts and photocatalytic hosts.
## 4.1.1 Semiconductor@MOFs
The two-electron process of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction into CO has been widely investigated due to the relatively low kinetic barrier. When the Co-ZIF-9/ZIF-8 was integrated with graphitic carbon nitride \(\left(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\right)\), the composite photocatalyst can coordinate efficiently to promote the conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO and \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\) with visible-light illumination under mild reaction condition, in which the Co-ZIF-9 acts as a cocatalyst to promote the concentration of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at the photocatalytic active sites as well as the separation of light-induced carriers [45, 46]. As for CdS semiconductor as a photocatalyst, Co-ZIF-9 cocatalyst can also promote the photoreduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO. Moreover, the C source in CO production is also verified by using \({}^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) isotopic experiment. The apparent quantum yield was calculated to be \(1.93\%\) under \(420\ \mathrm{nm}\) monochromatic irradiation. The uniqueness of Co-ZIF-9 should be ascribed to the enhanced electron transfer according to the in-situ photoluminescence and photocurrent generation tests [47]. The in-situ synthesized composite of Co-ZIF
\(9/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) also showed definite activity for photoreduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) owing to the promoted charge separation and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) activation [48]. The electrostatic self-assembled carbon nitride nanosheet@UiO-66 zirconium-based MOF showed a high photocatalytic activity for the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to CO under visible light driven reduction. Compared with the bare carbon nitride nanosheets, the introduction of UiO-66 can endow the hybrid with strong abilities for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture. Moreover, the presence of UiO-66 can also transfer the photo-generated electron from to the photoexcited carbon nitride nanosheet to substantially inhibit the recombination of electron-hole pair, thus providing long-lived electrons for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction [49]. When the UiO-66 was further modified with light-harvesting \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) tetra(4-carboxylphenyl)porphyrin (CuTCPP), the resultant \(\mathrm{CuTCPP} \subset \mathrm{UiO-66/TiO}_{2}\) \(\left(\mathrm{CTU/TiO}_{2}\right)\) exhibited an optimal evolution rate of \(31.32\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction into CO, which is about seven times higher than that of pure \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). The combination of efficacious separation of photo-induced carries between MOF and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and the enhanced light absorption originating from chemically implanted Cu-porphyrin should be responsible for the promoted photocatalytic activity [50]. In addition, \(\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S@UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) composite was also prepared for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)
photocatalysts with a relatively low \( \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH} \) production rate of 6.8 \( \upmu \mathrm{mol} \cdot \mathrm{h}^{-1} \cdot \mathrm{g}^{-1} \) [51]. Similar strategies were also found in \( \mathrm{Zn}_{2} \mathrm{GeO}_{4} / \mathrm{Mg} \cdot \mathrm{MOF} \cdot 74 \) and other semiconductors/metal nanostructure@MOFs photocatalytic systems for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) photocatalytic conversion [52]. The \( \mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \mathrm{MOF} @ \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) core-shell was used as a proof of concept to demonstrate the effects of semiconductor and MOF. Ultrafast spectroscopy indicated that the photo-generated charge can effectively transfer from photoexcited inorganic \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) to \( \mathrm{Cu} \cdot \mathrm{MOF} \), thus providing long-lifetime electrons for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction adsorbed in the nonporous MOF core [53]. Recently, \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} @ \mathrm{MOF} \) (MIL-101) was prepared through accurately filling MOF with \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) nanoparticles and exhibited significant performance improvements in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) photoreduction (Figs. 5(a) and 5(b)) [42]. \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) nanoparticles play the role of light-absorbing/electron-generating units. The synergy between \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) nanoparticles and catalytic metal clusters in the matrix of MIL-101 facilitates photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) conversion. The photocatalyst named as \( 42\% \cdot \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \)-in-MIL-101-Cr-NO\(_{2}\) that consists of \( 42\% \cdot \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) in a MIL-101 showed a very high apparent quantum efficiency of \( 11.3\% \) at a wavelength of \( 350 \, \mathrm{nm} \) for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) photocatalytic reduction (Fig. 5(c)). The growth of \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) units with a precise regulation of spatial positioning in specific pores of MOF is the key to the eye-catching catalytic performance. As an example of metal oxide semiconductor encapsulated in MOF, \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) nanowires@MOF, i.e., \( \mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) (BTC=1,3,5-benzene tricarboxylate) demonstrated an enhanced photocatalytic efficiency for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) photoreduction to \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) (Fig. 5(d)) [43]. Such MOF can obviously enhance the stability of \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) by suppressing the water vapor-triggered corrosion. In addition, the photo-generated electron transfer from the conduction band of \( \mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) nanowires to the LUMO level of \( \mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2} \) is responsible for the photocatalytic activity promotion. In order to promote selective photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \)-to- \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) -some dual-metal sites incorporated
MOF photocatalysts have been developed. MOF-808-CuNi exhibited a high production rate of \( 158.7 \, \upmu \mathrm{mol} \cdot \mathrm{g}^{-1} \cdot \mathrm{h}^{-1} \) for \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) production (Fig. 5(e)). The synergy effect and self-adaptive phenomenon of dual-metal-site pairs play important role in stabilizing the diverse \( \mathrm{C}_{1} \) intermediates of \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) pathway (Fig. 5(f)). If MOF photocatalysts with dual-metal sites can combine the properties of nanoparticles to build a heterogeneous interface, it is expected that the performance will be further improved (Figs. 5(g) and 5(h)) [44]. Moreover, \( \mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{BDC}) \cdot \mathrm{MOF} \) coated CdS composite catalyst demonstrated an enhanced activity for photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction. In this study, the MOF coating was firstly used as a reaction platform for bridging heterogeneous photosensitizer CdS and molecular co-catalysts [Co(bpy)]\(^{2+}\). The charge transfer mechanism at the interface in the composite system was clarified through a series of spectroscopic and kinetic characterizations, providing a new idea for the design of a novel and efficient photocatalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction reaction system [54]. Recently, the photothermal catalysis that can couple photocatalysis and photothermal conversion is becoming more and more popular. The synergy can enhance the utilization of infrared light and promote the rate of charge separation and transport of materials, thus greatly improving the efficiency of catalytic reactions. A UiO-66 embedded with nanoparticles \( \mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3} \) was prepared by bulk-doping. Upon the irradiation of UV–vis–IR light, the \( \mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3} / \mathrm{UiO} \cdot 66 \) demonstrate a thermally-assisted photo-catalytic behavior for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction into \( \mathrm{CO} \) at a high rate of \( 25.60 \, \upmu \mathrm{mol} \cdot \mathrm{g}^{-1} \cdot \mathrm{h}^{-1} \), in which the MOF can enhance the \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption capacity while the high efficiency of photo-thermal conversion should promote rapid charge transfer for the separation of photo-generated electron-hole pairs. The new strategy that integrates the photocatalysis and photothermal assisted effect into one nanostructure will open up new vistas for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) photocatalytic reduction [55].
## 4.1.2 Metal@MOFs
The Au nanostructure@MOF nanosheet, i.e., pillared layered porphyrin paddle-wheel framework-3 (PPF-3) nanosheet, was designed to promote charge transfer as well as mass transport in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction (Fig. 6(a)). The organic chromophores has small cross-section for optical absorption, while the presence of Au can enhance the light absorption ability by the electrostatic interaction with PPF-3. In addition, the plasmon resonance of Au nanoparticle will induce energy transfer towards PPF-3 nanosheet (Fig. 6(b)). In the case of optimal thickness of PPF-3 nanosheets, the activity of \(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{PPF}-3\) nanosheet demonstrate about 4-fold
higher than that of pristine PPF-3 nanosheets for photo-catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion into HCOOH (Fig. 6(c)) [56]. The effects of different metal nanoparticles (Pt and Au) on photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion have also compared. The Au nanoparticle in \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - MIL-125(Ti) can trigger the hydrogen spillover and lead to the formation of \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) atom at the bridging oxygen linked, which exhibited a promoted activity for formate production. By contrast, the presence of Au nanoparticle in \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-125(Ti) is detrimental to the reaction. The promoting effect of hydrogen spillover induced by Pt nanoparticle was firstly documented [58]. Moreover, covalently attached photoactive centers \((\mathrm{Re}^{\prime}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\) (BPYDC)Cl, \(\mathrm{BPYDC}=2,2^{\prime}\) -bipyridine-5,5'-di-carboxylate) can be
quantitatively and precisely controlled within a zirconium MOF, UiO-67 [59]. The covalent interaction between Re photocatalyst and UiO-67 can prevents deactivation induced by the dimerization of the molecular catalyst. Especially, when plasma Ag nanoparticles are embedded in Re-UiO-67, the intensified near-Ag-surface electric fields can boost the spatially confined Re photoactive centers, thus leading to a seven-fold promotion in the conversion \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO under visible light (Fig. 6(d)) [57].
## 4.1.3 Photosensitizer@MOFs
The strategies of modifying the composition and structure of functional groups and nodes alone has limited improvement on the light absorption for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction of MOFs. Transition-metal ions complexes normally can serve as electron-transport chains due to the existence of multiple metal redox states and organic ligands. When cooperated with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) activator and light harvester, the pathway of high-energy intermediates will be avoided and the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction will be accelerated. The transition-metal ions (Co, Ni, Mn, Cu, etc.) complexes have been reported to function as cocatalysts for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction through enhancing charge-separation as well as surface reaction kinetics. The combination of chemical redox and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-trapping functions in framework substances is very attractive. Ruthenium-based photosensitizer@Co-ZIF-9 exhibited a very high catalytic turnover number (TON) of \(\sim450\) within \(2.5\,\mathrm{~h~}\) under mild illumination conditions for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion to CO. In this system, Co-ZIF-9 acted as a strong porous cocatalyst for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture and concentration [60]. The combination of photosensitizer \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{dmb})_{3}]^{2+}\) (dmb=4,4'-dimethyl-2,2'-bipyridine) and manganese bipyridine complex incorporated Zr-MOF demonstrated an efficient catalysis activity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to formate under visible-light irradiation. The sample showed a TON of about 110 in \(18\,\mathrm{h}\) superior to the homogeneous reference [61]. Moreover, the integration of photosensitizer \(\left([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}]_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{OH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) with high nuclear \(\mathrm{Co}_{6}\)-MOF also showed a promoted photocatalytic activity in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to CO through the electron transfer from photosensitizer to MOF [62].
## 4.1.4 Quantum dots@MOFs
Most recently, lead halide perovskite (LHP) nanocrystals have also
been actively pursued as photocatalysts due to their proper energy band structures, high tolerance of defect, long lifetime of photogenerated carrier, and excellent visible-light responses [63, 64]. The integration of LHP nanocrystals and graphene oxide or g\(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) have been explored for photocatalytic reduction \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) by facilitated charge separation. However, the LHP nanocrystals lack of satisfactory stability low \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capturing ability and effective catalytic sites, thus hindering further performance improvement of for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photocatalytic reduction. A new strategy that encapsulates \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) (MAPbI3) quantum dots in the nanopores of Fe-porphyrin-based MOFs was developed for visible-light-driven \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction (Fig. 7) [63]. Taking advantages of the protection of the MOF framework, the photocatalyst exhibited much higher stability even in water-containing reaction condition. In addition, the photogenerated electrons can be well separated from the confined \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) quantum dots to the close contact Fe catalytic sites, thus leading to a remarkably promoted photocatalytic activity for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to CO and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\). The total yield is as high as \(1,559\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) about 38-fold of that of pure MOF without perovskite quantum dots. The perovskite quantum dots@MOFs indicated an effective approach to improve the stability of quantum dots and facilitate the charge separation efficiency of MOF-based photocatalysts [64]. The halide perovskite \(\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\) quantum dots@ZIF composite was also prepared for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction to CO and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) through a facile in situ synthesis process of direct growth/coating of ZIF on quantum dots. The moisture stability, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture ability, and photo-generated charge separation efficiency of composite photocatalyst were simultaneously largely improved. The role of Co centers in ZIF-67 is unique catalytic active sites for further accelerating charge separation and activating \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules [63].
## 4.1.5 Graphene@MOFs
Ultrathin MOFs nanolayers/nanosheets were widely reported to expose rich readily accessible active sites and facilitate electron transfer/mass transport, thereby ensuring superior performance for many catalytic applications. Moreover, ultrathin MOFs
nanolayers/nanosheets usually represent accurate atomic space structure which can be used as an ideal model for verifying structure-activity relationships [65, 66]. However, the reports about the synthesis of ultrathin MOFs nanolayers/nanosheets-based photocatalyst were very limited due to the high surface energy. Coulomb interaction guided 2D/2D MOF/rGO heterostructures have also been prepared for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction. The strong \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction at 2D/2D MOF/rGO the heterostructure can trigger boosted electrostatic charge transportation/transfer leading to abundant electron density at the isolated \(\mathrm{Ni-N}_{4}\) sites in 2D MOF. The photo-generated electron was noticeably harvested on the 2D rGO scaffold. As a result, the obtained CO evolution rate is as high as \(3.8\times10^{4}\,\upmu\mathrm{mol\cdot h^{-1}\cdot g_{\mathrm{film}}^{-1}}\), and the selectivity of \(91.74\%\) is also desired [66]. A new 2D/2D MOF/rGO the heterostructure was prepared through using GO nanosheets as both growth template and electron mediator. The MOF layer represented a uniform coating with a thickness of 1.5 nm about three metal coordination layers. The resultant 2D/2D MOF/rGO hybrid catalyst showed a very high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction activity featuring with \(3,133\;\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}M O L}\) CO yield and \(95\%\) CO selectivity. The well-exposed active sites in MOF layer and the highly efficient electron transfer are responsible for high performance. The above studies about 2D/2D MOF/rGO hybrid catalysts will also inspire the design of other types of 2D/2D photocatalyst (Fig. 8) [65].
## 4.1.6 POMs@MOFs
Polyoxometalates (POMs) own metal oxide polyhedra \((\mathrm{MO}_{x},x{=}5,\) 6) as basic structural unit consisted of transition metal cations and polyoxometalate polyanions, in which transition metal M normally has a high valence, such as \(\mathrm{W}^{\mathrm{VI}},\,\mathrm{Mo}^{\mathrm{VI}},\,\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{V}},\,\mathrm{Ta}^{\mathrm{V}},\) and \(\mathrm{Nb}^{\mathrm{V}}\) . POMs as a class of typical cluster compound were widely investigated for catalytic applications [67–69]. Taking advantages of the superior traits of POMs including the redox capability, welldefined active sites, tunable light absorption, reversible electrons acceptance and donation, the POMs can be used as photocatalysts, cocatalysts, photosensitizers, and multi-electron mediator in the application of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction [16, 70–75].
Further investigations on these hypotheses will be explored in future works. Encapsulating of POMs into sulfonic acid functionalized MIL-101-Cr is also reported to promote \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) photoreduction activity [77]. Ti-substituted POMs in POMs@HKUST-1 was verified a role of proton and electron reservoir for the activity promotion in photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO [78].
## 4.1.7 MOF@MOF
Very recently, a MOF@MOF homojunction (Co-MOF-3 nanostacks) with highly orientated stacked phase and well-defined junction interface was prepared through a hollow \(\mathrm{Au/Ag}\) nanocubes-directed process (Fig. 10). The two phases presented similar composition of cobalt porphyrin. Interestingly, the crystal structure and the energy level alignment are slightly different. Moreover, a straightforward observation of light-generated charge separation at the homojunction interface of the two stacked nanoplates stacks in Co-MOF-3 nanostacks was obtained through the integration of Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) under proper light illumination. Compared with the single MOF nanoplates, the Co-MOF-3 nanostacks exhibited about 2.5 times enhanced photocatalytic reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO in water with visible-light illumination. The activity can also stabilize at least 22 h. The over-whelming high selectivity to CO production is approaching \(100\%\) in water media, which surpassed the majority of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction photocatalysts [79].
## 4.2 Electrocatalysis
Electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is considered as the most representative strategy to transform \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules into high-valued products, such as CO, HCOOH, \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}\), and so on, due to its great advantages of easy processing, simple devices, and low energy
consumption [9, 10, 23, 80, 81]. Especially, electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) can be easily operated using simple devices through converting renewable intermittent power, such as solar and wind, etc. Unfortunately, the intrinsically thermodynamic stability and the low electron affinity make the conversion of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecule require to conquer high kinetic barrier [82]. In addition, the competitive \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution reaction tends to decrease the selectivity to aiming products [70]. Thus, the development of suitable \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) electrocatalysts is very important to achieve high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) efficiency and selectivity. In the recent decade, various types of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) electrocatalysts have been developed including metals/oxides/chalcogenides, metal-free materials, and MOFs, etc [9, 71, 83, 84]. By contrast, MOFs exhibit favorable \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption as well as well-defined structure metrics and catalytic environments suitable for mechanism investigation. However, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) electrocatalysis is multiple electron-proton transfer process. The low electrical conductivity and the weak electron-donating capability suppress the development of pure MOFs as \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) electrocatalysts. Because of the product diversity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), it is also challenging to improve the catalytic selectivity of MOF to \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) products. Therefore, integrating active component with MOFs to conquer the above issues should be an attractive method to promote the selectivity and efficiency of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\).
## 4.2.1 Metal@MOFs
For example, the introducing of functional units like copper nanoparticle or 1,10-phenanthroline can increase the selectivity of MOFs to generate HCOOH or CO. However, the selection of desired materials or MOFs and the exploration of powerful methods to integrate multi-functionality in MOFs for the efficient generation of high-valued products are still challenging and longsought-after. The Ag@Al-PMOF \(\left(\mathrm{[Al_{2}(O H)_{2}(T C P P)}\right]\) , \(\mathrm{TCPP\=}\)
For example, the introducing of functional units like copper nanoparticle or 1,10-phenanthroline can increase the selectivity of MOFs to generate HCOOH or CO. However, the selection of desired materials or MOFs and the exploration of powerful methods to integrate multi-functionality in MOFs for the efficient generation of high-valued products are still challenging and longsought-after. The Ag@Al-PMOF \(\left(\mathrm{[Al_{2}(O H)_{2}(T C P P)}\right]\) , \(\mathrm{TCPP\=}\) tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin) hybrids were synthesized with the intimate contact of organic and inorganic components. The Ag nanoparticles are well encapsulated in the Al-PMOF frameworks while keeping a close contact with the conductive substrate (Figs. 11(a) and 11(b)). Ag nanostructures are well recognized as a highly effective electrocatalyst for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction into CO while the competitive side-reaction hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is also notable. The interface between Ag and AlPMOF is verified to largely change the surface electronic structure of Ag through electron transfer from Al-PMOF to Ag, thus leading to suppression for HER but a promotion for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) to CO. With the coating of MOF layer, the enhanced mass transfer and increased structural stability of Ag were also observed (Fig. 11(c)) [85]. Very recently, Ag@UiO-66 MOF was prepared to demonstrate the extra role of MOF as chemical modularity in electrocatalysis (Fig. 11(d)). Non-catalytic UiO-66 MOF was assembled on Ag electrode, which served as a porous membrane to modulate the local chemical environment of active sites. The electrocatalytic activity and selectivity in Ag@UiO-66 MOF towards CO production were simultaneously promoted. Electrochemical analysis results indicated the double roles of MOF membrane as physical and electrostatic mediators to modulate the reactants delivery modes for the Ag catalytic sites. In addition, the secondary coordination sphere of active site can also be modified in MOF membrane to stabilize the key reaction intermediates for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) to CO (Fig. 11(e)). The reported preparation strategy is general and applicable to other different types of metal nanoparticles [86]. As for the Cu@NU-1000 MOF electrocatalyst, the pre-loading of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) sites in NU-1000 film was conducted via solvothermal deposition, and each \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) node of MOF can immobilize about \(4.0\,\mathrm{Cu}\). The electroactive Cu nanoparticles near the MOF/FTO interface were prepared through applying a negative potential. The Cu@NU-1000 MOF exhibited a Faradaic efficiency of \(31\%\) at \(-0.82\,\mathrm{~V~}\) vs. RHE with formate as reaction product. The crystallinity and morphology of Cu@NU-1000 also
were stable after a long-term electrocatalysis test. The diameter and spatial effects of Cu nanostructure \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{MOF}\) showed great potential manipulation on \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) remains a great challenge [87]. Recently, researchers found that the sizes of Cu nanoparticles with \({\sim}1.5\) and \({\sim}0.9\ \mathrm{nm}\) can be well manipulated by the pore diameters modulation of MOFs. The synthesis method can inspire the structure–property relationship in \(\mathrm{Cu@MOF}\) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) [88]. The Cu cluster@HKUST-1 was prepared through changing paddlewheel Cu dimer in MOF from symmetric to asymmetric motif using a simple thermal treatment. The surface coordination number of Cu clusters was judiciously controlled thus leading to a promoted catalytic selectivity, activity as well as efficiency for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) . The Faradaic efficiency (FE) of \({\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}\) production reached a record \(45\%\) for MOF-tuned Cu cluster catalysts [89]. The organic film such as pyridinium-based additives was also confirmed to greatly promote the selectivity of \(\mathrm{Ag}\) and \({\mathrm{Cu}}\) up to \({>}99\%\) Faradaic efficiencies for electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR},\) which is also shedding light on the design of metal@MOF \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) electrocatalysts [90].
## 4.2.2 Metal oxide@MOFs
Integrating Cu-based nanoparticles with conductive MOF is highly attractive in which the resultant electrocatalyst can combine the advantages of both. Two-dimensional conductive Cu-based MOF (CuHHTP) was prepared as the starting material to in situ construct \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}(111)\) quantum dots on the porous CuHHTP \(\mathrm{(Cu_{2}O@CuHHTP)}\) (Fig. 12). Operando infrared spectroscopy and theoretical calculations verified that the \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}(111)\) can stabilize \({}^{\star}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{{}^{\ast}O C H}_{3},\) the key intermediates that determine the \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) reaction pathway, through \({\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}(111)\) and hydrogen bonding. The selectivity to \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) is up to \(73\%\) . The highly conductive CuHHTP support can boost electron transfer thus leading to a desirable large current density [91].
## 4.2.3 POM@MOFs
Polyoxometalate-metalloporphyrin organic frameworks (POM@MOFs) were developed by integrating cluster and metalloporphyrin (Fig. 13) [21]. The polyoxometalate acted as an electron provider. The inherent macrocycle conjugated \(\pi\) -electron
system in metalloporphyrin can promote the electron conductivity. The composite exhibited superior Faradaic efficiency of \(>94\%\) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO conversion over a wide potential range from \(-0.8\) to \(-1.0\,\mathrm{V}\) . The turnover frequency is as high as \(1,656\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) and the material can stabilize for \(>36\,\mathrm{h}\) .
## 4.2.4 Nanocarbon@MOFs
A unique 3D anionic In-based MOF V11 with good solvent, pH, and thermal stability was prepared (Fig. 14) [92]. In addition, methylene blue molecules were introduced into the pores of V11 and the carbon nanoparticles were further prepared in situ by calcination to form carbon nanostructure@V11 catalysts. Compared with the low \(76.0\%\) \(\mathrm{FE_{HCOO}^{-}}\) at \(-0.84\,\mathrm{~V~}\) of pristine MOF V11, the new carbon nanostructure@V11 showed a significantly enhanced activity with a maximum \(\mathrm{FE_{HCOO}^{-}}\) of \(90.1\%\) at \(-0.84\,\mathrm{V}\) . The experimental results suggest that methylene blue-derived carbon particles can be used as promoters to improve the electrical conductivity of the material, expose more active sites and facilitate charge/mass transfer during the electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reaction. Importantly, the strategy demonstrated a good generality for other classical MOFs and may bring new inspiration for the development of efficient MOFs-based catalysts for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) .
## 4.2.5 Metallocene@MOFs
Metallocene \(\mathrm{(Fe/Co/Ni/Ti/Zr/HfCp_{2}}\) , Co means cyclopentadienyl) is featured with high stability and notable aromaticity. Especially, the d-orbits in central metal can trigger a big \(\pi\) -electron system of cyclopentadienyl implying that the metallocene can serve as electron-donating or carrying units. In recent years, metallocene has attracted great interest from nanostructure synthesis to catalytic applications [93–97]. In order to overcome the constraints of low electron conductivity and poor electron-donating capability of MOFs for electrochemical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reaction \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\right)\) , metallocene \(\mathrm{CoCp}_{2}@\mathrm{MOF-545-Co}\) was prepared (Fig. 15). The resultant catalyst exhibited outstanding \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) electrocatalysis activity with a high \(\mathrm{FE_{CO}}\) of \(97\%\) at \(-0.7\,\mathrm{V}\) . The encapsulated metallocene can induce the formation of strong binding-interaction with metalloporphyrin in MOF-545-Co, and
the role of metallocene as electron donator and carrier for promoting the continuity of electron transfer in MOF frameworks can be enhanced. Thus adsorption energy of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) on the \(\mathrm{CoCp}_{2}@\mathrm{MOF}-545-\mathrm{Co}\) was largely reduced leading to the enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) activity [93].
## 4.3 Photoelectrocatalysis
It is well known that porphyrin component in chlorophyll is the photosensitive core in the photosynthetic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion. A series of photo-coupled electrocatalysts of Au, Cu, and Co single atom loaded hollow Zr-porphyrinic-MOF (HNTM-Au/Cu/Co-SA) were prepared to simulate the structure of chlorophyll (Figs. 16(a) and 16(b)). The Au single atom uniformly immobilized into
HNTM-Au-SA while the pristine nanostructure of HNTM-Au-SA was still well retained. Without the porphyrinic anchoring sites, Au nanoparticles with sizes ranging from 5 to \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\) were
formed. Compared with Au nanoparticles sample, HNTM-Au-SA sample presented a high \(94.2\%\) CO Faradaic efficiency at \(-0.9\ \mathrm{V}\) and a notable turnover frequency of \(37.000\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\) at \(-1.1\ \mathrm{V}\). When
the light illumination was introduced, the overpotentials of HNTM-Au-SA, HNTM-Cu-SA, and HNTM-Co-SA exhibited positive shifts of 130, 100, and 20, mV, respectively, for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion, which is well correlated with their energy gap values (Figs. 16(c) and 16(d)). The findings strongly verified that both the size and the electron transfer pathway on metal center can be well manipulated by the porphyrin component in MOF [16, 75]. The design of p-type photocathode is very important for photoelectrochemical (PEC) conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into chemical fuels and feedstocks to address the energy and environmental challenges. The p-type \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) semiconductor was encapsulated into \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\)-MOF \(\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{MOF}\right)\) in order to overcome the photocorrosion problem of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). Encouragingly, the prepared \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{MOF}\) presented a notably promoted activity compared with the single \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in PEC conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to CO. Ultrafast spectroscopy confirmed the occurrence of greatly enhanced charge separation and transfer at the interface between \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and MOF. Moreover, The MOF coating can effectively promote both the activity and long-term stability of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) through preventing photocorrosion and offering more active sites for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) activation [98].
## 4.4 Thermal catalysis
Catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation into high-valued fuels and platform chemicals has great potential in constructing a sustainable carbon cycle [99, 100]. Owing to the abundant tenability in secondary building units of metal clusters and organic linkers, MOF has received great popularity as a tunable catalyst and/or support platforms for heterogeneous catalysis. The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation of MOFs-based catalysts for producing \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\), HCOOH, and alkyl formates has been widely investigated in recent years. Therein, the manipulation of C–C bond formation and the C–O bond retaining is scientifically challenging, which is the major hurdle in achieving a high selectivity to oxygenates, especially \(\mathrm{C}_{2+}\) products.
## 4.4.1 Metal@MOFs
\(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MOF}-5\) was prepared through impregnation method for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) methanation. The high specific surface area and large pore volume
immobilization of Pt nanoparticle on the surface of MOF. The resultant \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Au@Pd@MOF}\) composite exhibited high catalytic performance in the reverse water gas shift (RWGS) reaction, in which the inner core-shell \(\mathrm{Au@Pd}\) nanoparticles promote the CO selectivity while the surface Pt enhance the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion (Fig. 17(e)) [99]. The \(\mathrm{Cu@UiO-66}\) catalyst exhibited a promotion in the activity and selectivity of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation to methanol through strong interaction between \(\mathrm{Cu}\) and \(\mathrm{Zr}\) oxide \(\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(-\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}\right]\) units. The optimal 18 nm \(\mathrm{Cu}\) nanocrystal@UiO-66 \(\left(\mathrm{Cu} \subset \mathrm{LiO}-66\right)\) sample can produce a \(100\%\) selectivity for methanol and a about 8-fold enhanced yield compared with the benchmark \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) catalyst. The lower oxidation state of \(\mathrm{Zr}\) oxide units induced by the existence of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) are suggested to be responsible for high activity (Fig. 17(e)) [100, 104]. In addition, \(\mathrm{Cu}\) nanoparticles embedded in amorphous UiO-66 catalyst was fabricated by simple spray-drying method. Notably, the catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Cu@}\) amorphous UiO-66 in terms of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation to methanol was verified to be about threefold that of \(\mathrm{Cu@}\) crystalline UiO-66. The metal copper nanoparticles (4–6 and \(8{-}12\ \mathrm{nm}\)) incorporated HKUST-1 MOF thin films \(\left(\sim\!100\ \mathrm{nm}\right.\) thick) were firstly reported and the catalytic activity for methanol oxidation was straightforwardly investigated under vacuum-controlled surface science methods. The pure
HKUST-1 film without \(\mathrm{Cu}\) nanoparticles exhibited no catalytic activity for \(\mathrm{MeOH}\) oxidation reaction. The catalytic activity for methanol oxidation was found to be highly depending on the sizes of the copper nanoparticles, in which \(4{-}6\ \mathrm{nm}\) copper clusters can selectively generate formaldehyde molecules and the \(8{-}12\ \mathrm{nm}\) copper nanoparticles can produce extra \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) due to the formation of formate species. Moreover, the \(\mathrm{Cu-HKUST-1}\) interface sites have also been confirmed to stabilize the \(\mathrm{MeOH}\) molecules and inhibit aggregation during the continuous oxidative process [105]. Based on the full characterizations, \(\mathrm{Cu}\) nanoparticles are regarded as active sites for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) dissociation in the form of \(\mathrm{Cu-H}\) species. The process of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation to methanol occurs at \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\) clusters of UiO-66 by coupling \(\mathrm{CO}\) species and the spillover \(\mathrm{H}\). The reason for performance enhancement in \(\mathrm{Cu@}\) amorphous UiO-66 should be attributed to the existence of a larger number of open \(\mathrm{Zr}\) sites, which can not only promote the stabilization of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) nanoparticles, but also enhance \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) dissociation and \(\mathrm{H}\) spillover [106]. The above researches provide in-depth insights into the understanding about the roles of nanoparticles and open nodes in regulating the formation of active hydrogen and intermediate species, respectively, in the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation reaction for nanostructure@MOF structures. Moreover, a series of \(\mathrm{Cu@}\) \(\gamma\)-\(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF-8}\), \(\mathrm{Cu@MIL-100}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu@UiO-66}\) composites were compared in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation to methanol reaction to study the relationship between charge transfer dependence and catalytic activity. By contrast, UiO-66 is verified to be the best support for \(\mathrm{Cu}\), and the defects of \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\)-clusters in UiO-66 showed invisible effects on the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation performance. Compared with \(\mathrm{Cu@}\) \(\gamma\)-\(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) reference, the \(\mathrm{Cu@Zr}-\mathrm{UiO-66}\) produced a 70 times higher methanol generation rate. When \(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\) was replaced with \(\mathrm{Hf}^{+}\), \(\mathrm{Cu@Hf}-\mathrm{UiO-66}\) can further triple for the methanol production rate. The charge transfer from \(\mathrm{Cu}\) nanoparticles to UiO-66 support was suggested to be responsible for the activity enhancements of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) [18, 107, 108].
## 4.4.2 Metal oxide@MOFs
Although metal nanostructure@MOFs exhibited attractive \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation catalytic activities, non-noble metal oxide@MOFs have also been studied. The \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) @two types of ZIF-8 and MIL-53(Al) were prepared for catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation. The MOFs structures are stable under the reaction conditions of \(3\ \mathrm{MPa}\) and 573 K. Owing to the great \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption property, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) @MOFs showed superior catalytic activity compared with traditional \(\mathrm{g}-\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) support. Interestingly, ZIF-8 and MIL-53(Al) produced different effects on the catalytic activity and the selectivity. The acidity of MIL-53(Al) led to the activity enhancement of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation and the extremely high selectivity to alkanes. By contrast, ZIF-8 has the superior ability for hydrogen storage and the potential rich \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) in the pores of MOF, which should promote the secondary hydrogenation reaction. As a result, the ZIF-8 showed a high selectivity towards light olefins. The finding implied that the properties of inner metal oxide particles can be well modulated by different types of MOFs supports [109]. The catalytic conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) via hydrogenation pathway is a very important way to produce clean methanol fuel. Inspired by the crucial roles of \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) interfaces in industrially used ternary \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) catalyst for methanol production, \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}\) nanoparticles anchored UiO-bpy MOF was prepared, in which the strong metal-support interaction (SMSI) between metal-oxo clusters and \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}\) nanoparticles and the confinement effect by the rich porosity of MOF were reported to be responsible for the enhanced catalytic activity and selectivity of \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}\) @UiO-bpy MOF (Fig. 18) [25].
## 4.4.3 Cluster@MOFs
In addition to metal or metal oxide nanoparticles, highly active
sub-nanometer metal clusters were also successfully encapsulated into MOFs frameworks for catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation. Supporting sub-nanometer metal clusters on the highly porous MOF matrix has been verified to be an effective method to overcome the synthesis and stabilization challenges. Dinuclear \(\mathrm{Pt}_{2}{}^{0}\) clusters@MOFs catalyst with well-controlled atomicity, electronics and structure was prepared through a thioether functionalized MOF channels (Fig. 19). The formation of \(\mathrm{Pt}_{2}{}^{0}\) dimers should go through mutual proximity of two neighboring \(\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}\) -S metals anchored at flexible thio-ether groups inside the pores of MOF during \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) reduction process. X-ray absorption fine structure spectrum (EXAFS) of \(\mathrm{Pt}_{2}{}^{0}\) clusters@MOFs indicated a 2.65 Å distance between \(\mathrm{Pt}^{0}{-}\mathrm{Pt}^{0}\) . \(\mathrm{Pt}_{0}{}^{2}\) dimers are also suggested to own
\(\mathrm{Pt-}\mathrm{O}\) distances of 2.79 and \(2.80\mathrm{~\AA~}\) through weakly interacting with oxamate moieties. As for the hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to methane reaction, activation energy of \(\mathrm{Pt}_{2}{}^{0}\) clusters@MOFs is 35 \(\mathrm{KJ{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}\) , which is much lower than \(65\,\mathrm{KJ{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}\) . Therefore, the \(\mathrm{Pt}_{2}{}^{0}\) clusters@MOFs can allow the hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to methane reaction to occur at low temperature \((<140\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . The multi-gram scale synthesis of \(\mathrm{Pt}_{2}{}^{0}\) clusters@MOFs as well as the high loading amount \((8\mathrm{\wt.\%})\) of \(\mathrm{Pt}_{2}{}^{0}\) is also very attractive for other catalytic methodology [110].
The \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}@\mathrm{Zr}_{12}\) -MOF was prepared to achieve the selective conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to ethanol through generating C-C bond while maintaining C-O bond intact. The active \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}^{1}\) sites were modulated by elaborately utilizing the secondary building units of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\) -MOF (Fig. 20). The introduction of extra alkali cations can not only finely tune the local structure of catalytic centres but also function an interesting promoters to obtain a notable ethanol selectivity as high as \({>}99\%\) and a high turnover number of 4,080 (operating conditions: supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) \(30\;\mathrm{MPa}\;\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) \(5\;\mathrm{MPa}\;\mathrm{H}_{2},\) \(85\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . Specially, alkali-metal promoters can generate an electronrich local environment for \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}^{1}\) sites to promote \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) activation as well as the formyl intermediate stabilization. The work not only suggested the novel strategy for bimetallic centres preparation but also the great promise of sub-nanometer active sites for catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation [32].
## 4.4.4 Molecular Complexe@MOFs
Ir polypyridine@UiO-type MOFs was developed for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation to formate. The Ir complex was incorporated into UiO-type MOFs in the form of isolated Ir site by utilizing proper ligands and -OH design on the 6-position. Interestingly, the catalysis was well achieved by replacing Ir polypyridine@UiO-type MOFs in a Soxhlet-type reflux-condensing system to effectively constructing a dynamic tri-phasic interface of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.\) - catalyst (Fig. 21(a)). Then the contact among the reactants was maximized by the dynamic gas/liquid/solid interface. The produced formic acid was removed to the refluxing flask by siphoning thus facilitating the positive movement of catalytic reactions \((\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{g})\ +\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}(\mathrm{g})\ =\ \mathrm{HCO}_{2}\mathrm{H}\) (aq)). A high turnover frequency of about \(410\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) was achieved in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation to formate under the operating conditions of atmospheric pressure and \(85\,^{\circ}\!C\) (Fig. 21(b)). Then the contact among the reactants was
maximized by the dynamic gas/liquid/solid interface. The produced formic acid was removed to the refluxing flask by siphoning thus facilitating the positive movement of catalytic reactions \(\mathrm{(H_{2}(g)\ +\ C O_{2}(g)\ =\ H C O_{2}H}\) (aq)). A high turnover frequency of about \(410\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) was achieved in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation to formate under the operating conditions of atmospheric pressure and \(85~^{\circ}\!C\) (Fig. 21(b)). It is well-known that the \(\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{D}\) kinetic isotope effects (KIEs) measurements have not been well implemented in in homogeneous systems due to the masking effect of base-catalyzed \(\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{D}\) exchange. In this work, the notable KIEs of Ir polypyridine@UiO-type MOFs and the DFT calculations strongly verified that the rate-determining step was promoted through the intensive proton-hydride transfer via quantum tunneling (Fig. 21(c)). The work also suggests that the immobilization of molecular complexes into MOFs is a highly effective strategy to reveal the obscure reaction mechanism research in homogeneous systems [111].
## 5 Elucidation of structure-activity relationship
As we all know, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction process is highly complex due to the existence of the transfer of multiple protons and electrons, which can proceed through concerted proton-electron transfer (CPET), sequential proton-electron transfer (SPET), and hydride
transfer mechanisms. The initial activation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), the electrocatalysts' mesostructure (particle size, crystal facet, morphology, pore, grain boundaries, etc.), the reaction/process conditions (local \(\mathrm{pH}\), cationic species, current density, mass transport, etc.), and the formation of C-C bond can greatly affect the selectivity and activity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). The formation of various products \(\left(\mathbf{C}_{1/2/3}\right.\) molecules) generally goes through different reaction pathways and intermediates [9, 10]. Understanding the origin of activity and selectivity in heterogeneous \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction under real working conditions through operando techniques is very important to the development of closed-loop anthropogenic carbon cycle. The operando results obtained from X-ray, optical and electron-based techniques can offer many vital experimental evidence to address the five key questions in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction including the identification of catalytically active sites, the identification and configuration analysis of key intermediates, the origin of catalyst stability, the identification of reaction pathways, and the role of reaction environment (Fig. 22) [11, 112–114]. The surface-enhanced infrared adsorption spectroscopy (SEIRAS) and surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS) spectroscopy has been applied for the understanding of the metal nanoparticles and MOF interfaces (metal NP@MOF). The interfacial structure is found to be highly related to the preparation methods such as impregnation, coating, and one-pot process. The resultant direct
nanoparticles-MOF interface deriving from one-pot method exhibited the desired selectivity toward crotyl alcohol. By contrast, the residual trapped capping agent in other synthetic method will result in a reduced crotyl alcohol selectivity for the hydrogenation reaction. The spectroscopic protocol is verified to be an effective approach to reveal the exact role of the direct or indirect nanoparticles-MOF interface for catalytic application [37]. Moreover, the thermodynamical and/or kinetical evolutions of nodes and organic likens in MOFs for reaction intermediates stabilization and reorganization energy modulation are also very important for advanced catalyst design [114, 115]. Specifically, SEIRAS and SERS are two frequently used in-situ/operando spectroscopy techniques, which can well identify the species and configuration of surface-adsorbed intermediates. The \( \mathrm{CO} \), \( \mathrm{CHO} \) or \( \mathrm{COOH} \) and \( \mathrm{H} \) intermediates have been well monitored by SEIRAS to reveal the possible process in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). Moreover, SEIRAS can provide evidence for \( \mathrm{COCOOH} \), and the difference in C-O stretching frequencies sheds light on the formation of \( \mathrm{C}_{2} \) production. The effects of low pH on the production of CO at Au
electrode surface was also probed by in situ SEIRAS. The combination of in situ IRAS and SERS achieved the monitoring of \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \) in non-aqueous solvent system [11]. Very recently, in situ synchrotron-based techniques such as X-ray absorption spectroscopy and ambient-pressure X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy also aroused great interest in investigating the surface and interface electrochemistry in \( \mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR} \). The findings through operando techniques can promote the machine-learning-aided prediction of viable catalyst candidates for \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction. The gap between operando spectroscopy operation conditions and the actual catalytic environment should be continually narrowed [112].
## 6 Conclusions and perspectives
The review summarizes the recent advances of nanostructure@MOFs in catalytic \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) reduction on the basis of photocatalysis, electrocatalysis, photoelectrocatalysis, and thermal catalysis. Despite the fact that the great progress of emerging nanostructure@MOFs have been witnessed, this research field is
still at the early stage. According to the functions of MOFs in nanostructure@MOFs catalysts, we believe that the following challenges are urgently needed to be addressed. Firstly, the synthesis of confined low-dimension nanostructures with well-defined size, structure and spatial arrangement in MOFs is highly desired. Secondly, MOFs are usually serving as a front-loaded \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capturing materials to concentrate/enrich \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules for catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction. Then the pore structure and microenvironment (such as hydrophobicity, node and ligand) should be directionally modulated to achieve \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-specific adsorption from other competing gas molecules. The dynamic adsorption and desorption of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in MOFs are also attractive to investigate the mass transport and reaction balance in nanostructure@MOFs catalysts. Thirdly, in-depth understanding of the effects of interfacial structure and microenvironment of the active sites on the regulation of adsorption and desorption energies of reaction molecules/intermediates/products is an important prerequisite for revealing the structure-activity relationship in nanostructure@MOFs catalysts, which is also very urgent for the development of next-generation nanostructure@MOFs catalysts. The reaction pathway/mechanism of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction is featured with multi-step proton and electron transfer, which involves various reaction intermediates related to multiple products. To decouple the complex process of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction, integrating advanced operando characterization techniques with free energy calculations especially under real operating conditions is highly desirable. Last but not the least, more endeavors are still highly required to systematically promote the stability, selectivity, and activity of nanostructure@MOFs for catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion. In addition, the integration of two or all of the three types of photo/electro/thermal catalysis is emerging as a synergic enhancement system for catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion. All in all, nanostructure@MOFs materials are already presenting great promise as next-generation systems for catalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion. The new perspectives will stimulate the rapid development of nanostructure@MOFs materials to largely fulfill the requirements for practical catalytic applications in the near future.
